## DESCRIPTION
## Optimization with constraints
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Optimization with constraints')

################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "MathObjects.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

BEGIN_PGML


A company sells two different beverages: Mr. Salt and Valley Dry. It costs them

>>[` C(x,y) = 10x^{0.8} + 15y^{0.7} + 2,000,000 `]<<

to produce [` x `] cans of Mr. Salt and [` y `] cans of Valley Dry. They sell each can for [` \$2 `]. Currently, they are spending [` \$25,000,000 `] on producing these two drinks.

(a) By solving a Lagrange multiplier problem, they calculated a multiplier [` \lambda `] and chose values [` x,y `] accordingly to maximize their profit. Set up the system and determine their choices [` x,y, `] and [` \lambda `].

Use software to compute the solutions. Round [` x `] and [` y `] to the nearest whole number without commas and [` \lambda `] to three significant figures.

[`x = `][________]{Real(88864620)} cans.

[`y = `][________]{Real(492966)} cans.

[`\lambda \approx `][________]{Real(9.72)}.

(b) If they increase their production budget to [` \$26,000,000 `], estimate how much their maximum possible profit increases using the solution from the previous part. Do not solve another Lagrange multiplier system.

The maximum possible profit will increase by $[________]{Real(9.72)} million.

(c) How much does the maximum profit actually increase? Compare with your estimate above.

The maximum possible profit actually increases by $ insert number here million.
END_PGML

################################################################
#
#  Answers

$showHint = 0;
BEGIN_HINT
$BR
(b) Interpret the Lagrange multiplier.
$BR
(c) Solve another Lagrange multiplier system with the new production budget.
END_HINT

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION


(a) The Lagrange multiplier system in this case is:

>>[` \begin{align*} 2 &= \frac{8(\lambda-1)}{x^{0.2}}\\
    2 &= \frac{10.5(\lambda-1)}{y^{0.3}}\\
	10x^{0.8} + 15y^{0.7} &= 23,000,000 \end{align*} `]<<

Solving for [` x `] in terms of [` y `] and subsituting into cost gives the equation [` \frac{40}{5.25}y^{1.2} + 15y^{0.7} = 23,000,000 `]. Using a computer, we find that the only positive solution of this, rounded to the nearest unit, is [` y = 492,966 `] cans. The corresponding value of [` x `] is [` x = 88,864,620 `] cans, while the corresponding value of [` \lambda `] is [` \lambda \approx 9.72 `].

(b)	The change in maximum profit if we increase our spending by [` \$1,000,000 `] is approximately [` \frac{dM}{dc}1,000,000 `]. From the question, we know that [` \frac{dM}{dc} = \lambda `]. So, we approximate that profit will increase by [` \$9.72 `] million.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();